Summary of rules:

R1: Throw TypeError if text is not a string. Escape `&`, `<`, `>` everywhere (including inside code). All other characters pass through as-is.

R2: `*x*` → `<b>x</b>`, `_x_` → `<i>x</i>`, `` `x` `` → `<code>x</code>`.

R3: `*` or `_` only opens a span if the character immediately after exists and is not whitespace. The closer is the first later marker of the same kind whose preceding character is not whitespace, and which is not the character immediately after the opener (content must not be empty). A marker with no closer is plain text. Also, an opener that cannot open is plain text.

R4: `_` has an additional condition: it opens only if the preceding character is the start of text or a non-alphanumeric (A-Z a-z 0-9). It closes only if the following character is the end of text or a non-alphanumeric. So `snake_case_name` is plain text. `*` has no such condition.

R5: Backtick opens a code span that ends at the next backtick. Inside it only R1 escaping: no markup, no backslash handling. A backtick with no later backtick is plain text. Two backticks adjacent with nothing between are plain text (as two backticks). Code span takes precedence: when looking for the closer of a `*` or `_` span, a complete code span is skipped over, so a marker inside a code span can never be the closer.

R6: The content of `*` or `_` spans is rendered by the same rules (nested). A span ends at the first valid closer, so spans never overlap.

R7: A backslash followed by `*`, `_`, backtick, or another backslash outputs that second character as plain text (the backslash is dropped), and that character is not a marker and can never be a closer. A backslash anywhere else, and any backslash inside a code span, is plain text.

